From: TANYA@alcor.org
Date:  Tue, 27 Sep 94 12:46:57 
Subject: [#94-9-556] Cryonics and Metabolic Dependent State Loss

Phil Safier asked:
>Has anyone volunteered (or volunteered someone) to undergo 
>cryonic suspension for, say, 2 years and then defrost?  Surely it 
>takes only a little conspiracy ideation to imagine that THEY have 
>already answered some of the questions posed here.

As a matter of fact, a few people have volunteered to be frozen and 
revived (in the interest of science, of course).  No existing cryonics 
organization is able to accept cases like this for several reasons.  
The damage done to tissue during the cryonic suspension procedures 
is sufficient that no patient suspended with current techniques is 
revivable.  Not now, and not for many years to come.  (I wont 
speculate here on clandestine operations carried by hordes of 
doctors with ultra-advanced technology for wealthy individuals.)  
Another reason is that no patient may be accepted by a cryonics 
organization until they are pronounced legally dead.  Anything else 
would be murder, with the patient subjected to autopsy, which would 
reduce the individuals chances of revival even further.  Also, many 
people who volunteer for this sort of experiment do not understand 
the realities of cryonics, as it is practiced today.  An ethical 
organization is reluctant to accept patients who are unwilling or 
unable to consider rationally the choices that are involved in 
choosing to be cryonically suspended.  To extend this even further, 
such a volunteer might be physically, but not psychologically, 
healthy.  A cryonic suspension, when carried out without a clear 
terminal course, would (once again) result in autopsy.  Not to mention, 
the family would probably sue.

>How long has a dog stayed down before resuscitation? Did the dog 
>remember its name and tricks?

Experimentation has resulted in animals being cooled to just above 
freezing, held there for nearly six hours, rewarmed, and revived with 
little observable neurologic damage.  Those with names remembered 
them.  (Research animals are bred in rather sterile and closed 
environments.  Many dogs, for example, have never seen the "great 
outdoors" in their lives.  They also have numbers, not names.)

>If you answer that none of the above has happened, i want to ask 
>how do you know that?

But it has happened:  just check out almost any copy of the Weekly 
World News!  ;-)

Doug Cutrell:
>It's not clear to me how low the body temperature
>would have to drop before we could be sure that all energy transport
>mechanisms had ceased. 

For those who want to do the calculatiions:  a temperature drop of 10 
degrees C reduces the metabolic rate by 50% over time (in people, 
Im not sure about other systems).  Cryonic suspension patients are 
ultimately stored at -196 degrees C.

from Phil Safier:
>Do those who think cryonics will allow future revival also 
>think that after physical death and decomposition personal 
>existence ends?

I think that cryonics may enable the future revival of an individual.  I 
also think that the current criterion for legal death (the cessation of 
heartbeat and breathing, in most cases) is wrong, with respect to 
revivability.  Im not complaining, mind you (at least, not in this 
context).  Most cryonic suspension patients are individuals who have 
experienced a course of illness which results in the cessation of 
heartbeat and breathing.  That illness could be anything from AIDS or 
cancer to old age.  For many of these individuals, current technology 
is capable of reviving them.  However, current technology is unable to 
restore a quality of life.  Few people find joy in life when bedridden, 
unable to communicate, requiring multiple injections daily, when they 
cant eat and are being fed through an intravenous line, etc.  "Quality 
time" is more than just a euphemism to someone who lacks it.

Decomposition complicates the matter, which is why great lengths are 
taken to prevent the degradation of structure in cryonic suspension 
patients.

>If freezing preserves the self, then it must come from the body and 
>cannot leave it. 

Yup.

--Tanya

